  "lineage": "Pre-contact and continuing repertoire across the Polynesian triangle — Tonga, Sāmoa, Hawaiʻi, Aotearoa, Tahiti and the Cooks — documented ethnographically from Cook's voyages onward and systematically from the mid-20th century by researchers such as Mervyn McLean and Adrienne Kaeppler: sung poetry over sharp-edged idiophone percussion (slit gong, sharkskin pahu, struck bamboo, body slaps), narrow-range recitational melody widening into stacked polyphony after mission contact, conch-shell calls framing ceremony, and rhythm organised in fast even subdivisions rather than harmonic cycles.",
